{
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why did MLK say 'I Have a Dream'?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Martin Luther King Jr. (1929\u20131968) was a Baptist minister and civil rights leader who led America's nonviolent civil rights movement. He delivered the 'I Have a Dream' speech at the 1963 March on Washington, helped win landmark civil rights laws, and received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1964."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why didn't Rosa Parks give up her seat?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Rosa Parks (1913\u20132005) was a seamstress and civil rights activist in Montgomery, Alabama. On December 1, 1955, she refused to give up her bus seat to a white passenger and was arrested. Her act sparked the 381-day Montgomery Bus Boycott, a turning point in the civil rights movement."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How did Harriet Tubman free enslaved people?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Harriet Tubman (c. 1822\u20131913) was born enslaved in Maryland, escaped to freedom, and risked her life returning about 13 times on the Underground Railroad to free roughly 70 enslaved people. She later served the Union Army in the Civil War as a scout, spy, and nurse."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How did Frederick Douglass learn to read?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Frederick Douglass (c. 1818\u20131895) escaped slavery and taught himself to read and write, skills enslavers tried to deny him. He became the most famous abolitionist speaker of his time and told his own story in his bestselling 1845 Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What did Sojourner Truth fight for?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Sojourner Truth (1797\u20131883) was born enslaved in New York, escaped with her infant daughter, and won freedom for her son in court. She became a traveling preacher and speaker for abolition and women's rights, famous for her 1851 speech remembered as 'Ain't I a Woman?'"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black President?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Barack Obama (born 1961) was the 44th President of the United States, serving from 2009 to 2017, and the first Black president in American history. His presidency passed the Affordable Care Act health law, and he received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2009."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black Vice President?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Kamala Harris (born 1964) served as Vice President of the United States from 2021 to 2025 \u2014 the first woman, first Black American, and first South Asian American to hold the office. She had previously been a U.S. Senator and California's Attorney General."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black Supreme Court Justice?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Thurgood Marshall (1908\u20131993) was the first Black justice on the U.S. Supreme Court, serving from 1967 to 1991. Before that, as a lawyer for the NAACP, he argued Brown v. Board of Education, the 1954 case that ruled school segregation unconstitutional."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is 'good trouble'?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "John Lewis (1940\u20132020) was a civil rights leader who helped lead the 1965 Selma voting rights march, where he was beaten on 'Bloody Sunday,' and later served Georgia in Congress for over 30 years. He called principled protest 'good trouble, necessary trouble.'"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black woman to run for President?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Shirley Chisholm (1924\u20132005) became the first Black woman elected to the U.S. Congress in 1968, representing Brooklyn, New York. In 1972 she ran for president \u2014 the first Black candidate to seek a major party's presidential nomination \u2014 under the slogan 'Unbought and Unbossed.'"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why did Malcolm X change his name?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Malcolm X (1925\u20131965) was a Black rights activist and minister who taught Black pride, self-reliance, and self-defense against racism. After leaving the Nation of Islam he broadened his message toward human rights; his Autobiography, published after his assassination, remains a classic."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who helped found the NAACP?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "W.E.B. Du Bois (1868\u20131963) was a sociologist, historian, and co-founder of the NAACP in 1909. He was the first Black American to earn a Ph.D. from Harvard, and his 1903 book The Souls of Black Folk is one of the most important works in American history."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How did Ida B. Wells use newspapers to fight injustice?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Ida B. Wells (1862\u20131931) was an investigative journalist who used newspapers and pamphlets to expose lynching in America when few dared to. She co-founded the NAACP, fought for women's voting rights, and ran much of her crusade from Chicago."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who refused to move on a bus before Rosa Parks, at 15?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Claudette Colvin (born 1939) was 15 years old in Montgomery, Alabama, when, on March 2, 1955 \u2014 nine months before Rosa Parks \u2014 she refused to give up her bus seat and was arrested. She later became a plaintiff in Browder v. Gayle, the case that ended bus segregation."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the 6-year-old who integrated her school?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Ruby Bridges (born 1954) was 6 years old in 1960 when she became the first Black child to integrate an all-white elementary school in the South, in New Orleans. Federal marshals escorted her past angry crowds, and she spent the year as the only child in her classroom."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How does voting work and why does it matter?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Stacey Abrams (born 1973) is a Georgia politician, lawyer, and voting rights activist who founded Fair Fight to protect voters' access to the ballot. In 2018 she became the first Black woman nominated for governor by a major party in U.S. history."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the first Black woman on the Supreme Court?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Ketanji Brown Jackson (born 1970) is the first Black woman \u2014 and the first former public defender \u2014 to serve on the U.S. Supreme Court, confirmed in 2022. Before the Court she was a federal judge and vice chair of the U.S. Sentencing Commission."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why did Fannie Lou Hamer say she was 'sick and tired'?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Fannie Lou Hamer (1917\u20131977) was a Mississippi sharecropper who became a voting rights leader after being fired and threatened for trying to register to vote. Co-founder of the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party, her 1964 televised testimony \u2014 'I'm sick and tired of being sick and tired' \u2014 shook the nation."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black woman in space?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Mae Jemison (born 1956) is a physician, engineer, and NASA astronaut. On September 12, 1992, aboard the Space Shuttle Endeavour, she became the first Black woman to travel into space. She later founded companies and programs to bring science education to students."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How did Katherine Johnson help NASA get to the Moon?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Katherine Johnson (1918\u20132020) was a NASA mathematician whose hand calculations of flight paths helped send John Glenn into orbit in 1962 \u2014 Glenn personally asked her to check the computer's numbers. Her story reached millions through the book and film Hidden Figures."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who led NASA's Black women 'human computers'?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Dorothy Vaughan (1910\u20132008) was a NASA mathematician and the agency's first Black supervisor, leading the West Area Computing group of Black women 'human computers.' Foreseeing electronic computers, she taught herself and her team FORTRAN and became an expert programmer."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was NASA's first Black woman engineer?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Mary Jackson (1921\u20132005) became NASA's first Black woman engineer in 1958, after petitioning for permission to take graduate classes at a segregated school. At Langley she studied wind tunnel and flight data to make aircraft safer, and later worked to open NASA careers to women."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What did Carver invent with peanuts?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "George Washington Carver (c. 1864\u20131943) was an agricultural scientist at Tuskegee Institute who taught Southern farmers crop rotation to restore worn-out soil. He developed hundreds of products from peanuts, sweet potatoes, and soybeans, becoming one of America's most famous scientists."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who invented the modern blood bank?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Charles Drew (1904\u20131950) was a surgeon and medical researcher who discovered how to store blood plasma safely for long periods. He organized the first large-scale blood banks during World War II, saving countless lives, and trained a generation of Black physicians."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who invented the traffic light and gas mask?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Garrett Morgan (1877\u20131963) was an inventor who patented a three-position traffic signal and a safety hood \u2014 an early gas mask \u2014 that he used to rescue workers trapped in a 1916 tunnel explosion under Lake Erie. He also founded a newspaper and a cosmetics company."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who built a clock and helped survey Washington, D.C.?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Benjamin Banneker (1731\u20131806) was a free Black, largely self-taught astronomer and mathematician in Maryland. He built a working wooden clock, published popular almanacs with his astronomical calculations, and helped survey the land that became Washington, D.C."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What does 'the real McCoy' mean?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Elijah McCoy (1844\u20131929) was a Canadian-born American engineer whose 1872 automatic lubricator let train engines oil themselves without stopping, a major advance for railroads and factories. His devices were so trusted that buyers asking for 'the real McCoy' became a popular saying."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who helped make the light bulb last longer?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Lewis Latimer (1848\u20131928) was an inventor and draftsman who drew the patent drawings for Alexander Graham Bell's telephone and improved Thomas Edison's light bulb with a longer-lasting carbon filament. He was a founding member of Edison's engineering team, the Edison Pioneers."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who invented a laser treatment to restore sight?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Patricia Bath (1942\u20132019) was an ophthalmologist who invented the Laserphaco Probe, a device that removes cataracts with a laser and restores sight. In 1988 she became the first Black woman physician in America to receive a medical patent."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who performed one of the first successful heart surgeries?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Daniel Hale Williams (1856\u20131931) was a pioneering heart surgeon who in 1893 performed one of the first successful surgeries on the human heart's lining, saving a stabbing victim. He founded Provident Hospital in Chicago, the first Black-owned hospital in America."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How do we know about black holes and the universe?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Neil deGrasse Tyson (born 1958) is an astrophysicist and America's best-known science communicator. Director of New York's Hayden Planetarium, he hosts Cosmos and StarTalk, explaining black holes, stars, and the universe to millions of readers and viewers."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Whose math made GPS possible?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Gladys West (1930\u20132026) was a mathematician at a naval base in Dahlgren, Virginia, whose precise mathematical modeling of the Earth's shape using satellite data became a foundation of GPS \u2014 the technology that now guides phones, cars, planes, and ships worldwide."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black American in space?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Guion Bluford (born 1942) is an aerospace engineer and Air Force pilot who, aboard the Space Shuttle Challenger in 1983, became the first Black American in space. He flew four shuttle missions and later worked in the aerospace industry."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why does the caged bird sing?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Maya Angelou (1928\u20132014) was a poet, author, and civil rights activist whose 1969 memoir I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings became a classic read in schools nationwide. In 1993 she read her poem 'On the Pulse of Morning' at a presidential inauguration."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What was the Harlem Renaissance?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Langston Hughes (1901\u20131967) was a poet and leading voice of the Harlem Renaissance, the 1920s flowering of Black art in New York. His poems, including 'Harlem' ('What happens to a dream deferred?'), captured Black everyday life in the rhythms of jazz and blues."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why do students still read James Baldwin?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "James Baldwin (1924\u20131987) was a novelist and essayist whose books \u2014 Go Tell It on the Mountain, Notes of a Native Son, and The Fire Next Time \u2014 explored race, identity, and America with unmatched honesty. Students still study his essays and debates today."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black woman to win the Nobel Prize in Literature?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Toni Morrison (1931\u20132019) was a novelist whose book Beloved, about a mother haunted by slavery, won the Pulitzer Prize. In 1993 she became the first Black woman to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, honored for novels of visionary force about Black American life."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who collected Black folktales in the South?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Zora Neale Hurston (1891\u20131960) was a novelist and anthropologist of the Harlem Renaissance who traveled the South collecting Black folktales, songs, and traditions. Her 1937 novel Their Eyes Were Watching God is now recognized as one of the great American novels."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first published Black poet in America?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Phillis Wheatley (c. 1753\u20131784) was kidnapped from West Africa and enslaved in Boston, where she learned to read and write poetry. Her 1773 book Poems on Various Subjects made her the first published African American poet \u2014 while she was still enslaved."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black Pulitzer Prize winner?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Gwendolyn Brooks (1917\u20132000) was a Chicago poet who in 1950 became the first Black author to win a Pulitzer Prize, for her book Annie Allen. As Illinois Poet Laureate she spent decades encouraging young writers, especially on Chicago's South Side."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What does Invisible Man mean?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Ralph Ellison (1914\u20131994) was a novelist whose 1952 book Invisible Man \u2014 about a Black man society refuses to truly see \u2014 won the National Book Award and is ranked among the greatest American novels of the 20th century."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who created modern Black science fiction?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Octavia Butler (1947\u20132006) was a science fiction writer who created Kindred, a time-travel novel about slavery, and the Parable series about a future America. She was the first science fiction writer to receive a MacArthur 'Genius' Grant."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who read a poem at the inauguration at age 22?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Amanda Gorman (born 1998) is a poet and activist who, at 22, became the youngest inaugural poet in U.S. history, reading 'The Hill We Climb' in 2021. Her performance made her an instant role model for young writers everywhere."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What are the best books for kids who don't like reading?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Jason Reynolds (born 1983) is a bestselling author of books for young readers, including Ghost and Long Way Down, written to reach kids who think they don't like reading. He served as National Ambassador for Young People's Literature."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who wrote Brown Girl Dreaming?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Jacqueline Woodson (born 1963) is an award-winning author whose memoir in verse, Brown Girl Dreaming, about growing up between South Carolina and Brooklyn, won the National Book Award. She has written beloved books for children, teens, and adults."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Can a novel be written in poems about basketball?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Kwame Alexander (born 1968) is a poet and author whose The Crossover \u2014 a novel about twin basketball players, written entirely in poems \u2014 won the 2015 Newbery Medal, children's literature's top honor. He champions poetry and reading for young people worldwide."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why is Beyonc\u00e9 called Queen Bey?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Beyonc\u00e9 (born 1981) is a singer, songwriter, and performer from Houston, Texas, and the most awarded artist in Grammy history. Her 2024 album Cowboy Carter, which reclaimed Black roots in country music, won Album of the Year and topped charts worldwide."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why did Kendrick Lamar perform at the Super Bowl halftime show?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Kendrick Lamar (born 1987) is a rapper from Compton, California, who in 2018 became the first popular musician to win the Pulitzer Prize for Music. He headlined the Super Bowl LIX halftime show in 2025, one of the most-watched performances in history."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the King of Pop?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Michael Jackson (1958\u20132009) was the 'King of Pop,' a singer, songwriter, and dancer who started with his brothers in the Jackson 5. His 1982 album Thriller is the best-selling album of all time, and his moonwalk and music videos changed pop music forever."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the Queen of Soul?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Aretha Franklin (1942\u20132018) was the 'Queen of Soul,' whose powerful gospel-trained voice powered hits like 'Respect' and 'Natural Woman.' She sang at civil rights events and presidential inaugurations, and in 1987 became the first woman inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How does Stevie Wonder make music?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Stevie Wonder (born 1950) is a singer-songwriter and multi-instrumentalist signed to Motown at age 11 despite being blind since infancy. His albums, including Songs in the Key of Life, are masterpieces, and he has won 25 Grammy Awards."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why did Prince change his name to a symbol?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Prince (1958\u20132016) was a Minneapolis musician, singer, and producer who played nearly every instrument on his albums. His album and film Purple Rain made him a superstar; in the 1990s he famously changed his name to a symbol while fighting for artists' rights."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who has one of the greatest voices ever?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Whitney Houston (1963\u20132012) was a singer known simply as 'The Voice,' one of the best-selling music artists of all time. Her hits include 'I Will Always Love You' from the film The Bodyguard, and her 1991 Super Bowl national anthem is considered the standard."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What's Going On - and why does it still matter?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Marvin Gaye (1939\u20131984) was a Motown soul singer whose 1971 album What's Going On asked hard questions about war, poverty, and the environment \u2014 and is now ranked among the greatest albums ever made. His duets and solo hits defined soul music."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How did Nina Simone mix jazz, classical, and protest?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Nina Simone (1933\u20132003) was a classically trained pianist and singer who fused jazz, blues, gospel, and classical music. Refused by a conservatory, she became 'the High Priestess of Soul' and wrote civil rights anthems like 'Mississippi Goddam.'"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who helped invent jazz?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Louis Armstrong (1901\u20131971) was a New Orleans trumpeter and singer who helped invent jazz as America's art form. His joyful playing, gravelly voice, and songs like 'What a Wonderful World' made him one of the most beloved musicians on Earth."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the First Lady of Song?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Ella Fitzgerald (1917\u20131996), the 'First Lady of Song,' was a jazz singer famous for her pure tone, three-octave range, and scat singing \u2014 improvising with nonsense syllables like an instrument. Her Song Book albums preserved America's greatest songwriting."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who led the most famous jazz orchestra?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Duke Ellington (1899\u20131974) was a composer, pianist, and bandleader who led his orchestra for nearly 50 years, from Harlem's Cotton Club to concert halls worldwide. He composed thousands of works, including 'It Don't Mean a Thing (If It Ain't Got That Swing).'"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why is SZA one of the biggest R&B stars today?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "SZA (born 1989) is an R&B singer-songwriter whose albums Ctrl and SOS made her one of the biggest stars in music today. SOS spent many weeks at No. 1, and her honest songs about love and self-worth connect strongly with young listeners."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who won Best Rap Album and went viral in 2025?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Doechii (born 1998) is a rapper and singer from Tampa, Florida, whose mixtape Alligator Bites Never Heal won the Grammy for Best Rap Album in 2025 \u2014 making her only the third woman ever to win that award. Her theatrical performances went viral worldwide."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How did a Black artist top the country charts?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Shaboozey (born 1995) is a singer who blends hip-hop and country music. His 2024 hit 'A Bar Song (Tipsy)' tied the all-time record with 19 weeks at No. 1 on the Billboard Hot 100, proving country music's Black roots are alive today."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How did Tyler build his own music and fashion world?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Tyler, the Creator (born 1991) is a Grammy-winning rapper and producer who co-founded the group Odd Future as a teenager, built his own fashion brand Golf Wang, and creates his albums as complete worlds. His 2024 album Chromakopia debuted at No. 1."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How did Megan balance college and rap stardom?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Megan Thee Stallion (born 1995) is a Houston rapper who coined 'Hot Girl Summer,' won the Grammy for Best New Artist in 2021, and \u2014 while topping charts \u2014 graduated from Texas Southern University in 2021, an HBCU achievement she celebrates often."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who headlined the Super Bowl and redefined R&B performance?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Usher (born 1978) is an R&B singer and dancer whose 2004 album Confessions became one of the best-selling albums of its decade. A star since his teens, he headlined the Super Bowl LVIII halftime show in 2024."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who played Black Panther and Jackie Robinson?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Chadwick Boseman (1976\u20132020) was an actor who played Black Panther, Marvel's King T'Challa of Wakanda, in a film that became a cultural phenomenon. He also portrayed Jackie Robinson, James Brown, and Thurgood Marshall. He died of cancer at 43, working through his illness in secret."
