   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "John Coltrane (1926\u20131967) was a saxophonist whose album A Love Supreme, a suite offered as a prayer, is one of the most revered recordings in any genre. His sheets-of-sound playing and spiritual searching transformed jazz in a short, intense career."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was 'Bird', the bebop saxophone genius?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Charlie Parker (1920\u20131955), nicknamed 'Bird,' was a Kansas City alto saxophonist who co-invented bebop in 1940s New York, playing at speeds and with harmonies no one had heard. Nearly every jazz musician since has studied his solos."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the piano genius behind 'Round Midnight'?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Thelonious Monk (1917\u20131982) was a pianist and composer with a percussive, angular style all his own, composer of 'Round Midnight,' the most recorded jazz standard by a jazz musician. Long underappreciated, he is now ranked beside Duke Ellington as a composer."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who sang 'Strange Fruit', the protest song about lynching?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Billie Holiday (1915\u20131959), nicknamed 'Lady Day,' was a jazz singer whose phrasing behind the beat changed singing itself. In 1939 she recorded 'Strange Fruit,' a haunting song about lynching that became one of the first great protest songs in American music."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
