    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Amara La Negra (born 1990) is a Dominican-American singer and TV star from Miami who uses her platform \u2014 including Love & Hip Hop: Miami \u2014 to insist Afro-Latinas belong at the center of Latin entertainment, challenging colorism in Latin media directly."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the Dominican rapper behind 'Patotinha' and dembow hits?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Tokischa (born 1996) is a Dominican rapper from Los Alcarrizos whose raw dembow and rap songs, and collaborations with Rosal\u00eda and Madonna, made her the most talked-about new voice in Dominican urban music. She champions women from her barrio on a global stage."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the Latina drummer-singer who played with Prince?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Sheila E. (born 1957) is an Oakland-born singer and percussionist of Mexican and Creole heritage who became famous playing drums and singing with Prince on 'The Glamorous Life.' A member of the Escovedo musical family, she is one of the world's great drummers."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black Oscar winner in 1940?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Hattie McDaniel (1893\u20131952) was an actress and singer who in 1940 became the first Black person to win an Academy Award, for playing Mammy in Gone with the Wind \u2014 accepting it at a segregated hotel where she sat at a side table. Her Oscar itself later went missing from Howard University."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black woman nominated for the Best Actress Oscar?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Dorothy Dandridge (1922\u20131965) was an actress and singer who in 1954 became the first Black woman nominated for the Academy Award for Best Actress, for Carmen Jones. A nightclub star before film fame, she helped open Hollywood's leading-lady roles to Black women."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who sang 'Stormy Weather' and integrated nightclubs?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Lena Horne (1917\u20132010) was a singer, actress, and activist whose elegance at MGM and on records like 'Stormy Weather' made her a star despite Hollywood's limits on Black actresses. She refused demeaning roles and marched for civil rights into old age."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the St. Louis dancer who became a Paris star and French Resistance hero?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Josephine Baker (1906\u20131975) was a St. Louis-born dancer and singer who became a superstar in Paris, then served France as a Resistance spy in World War II and refused to perform for segregated U.S. audiences. She spoke at the 1963 March on Washington in her French military uniform."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who played A Raisin in the Sun's mother on stage and screen?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Ruby Dee (1922\u20132014) was an actress who originated the role of Ruth in A Raisin in the Sun on Broadway and film, and with her husband Ossie Davis formed a first couple of Black theater and civil rights activism for six decades."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black woman to win the Best Actress Oscar?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Halle Berry (born 1966) is an actress who in 2002 became the first \u2014 and still only \u2014 Black woman to win the Academy Award for Best Actress, for Monster's Ball, dedicating it to the Black actresses who came before her. She also starred as Storm in the X-Men films."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who stars in Empire as Cookie Lyon?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Taraji P. Henson (born 1970) is an actress who plays Cookie Lyon in Empire and Katherine Johnson in Hidden Figures \u2014 the NASA mathematician from this project's first 100. A Howard University graduate, she founded a foundation for Black mental health in her late father's name."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who plays Olivia Pope in Scandal?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Kerry Washington (born 1977) is an actress who played Olivia Pope on Scandal, the first Black woman to headline a network TV drama in nearly 40 years when it premiered in 2012. She is also a producer and voting-rights activist."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who won an Oscar for The Help?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Octavia Spencer (born 1970) is an actress from Alabama who won the Academy Award for The Help and played NASA mathematician Dorothy Vaughan in Hidden Figures. She has earned three Oscar nominations and produces projects through her own company."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the singer-actress who plays Ashley Banks in Bel-Air?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Keke Palmer (born 1993) is an actress and singer who headlined Akeelah and the Bee at 11, became the youngest talk show host in TV history, and starred in Jordan Peele's Nope. Her books, podcasts, and hosting keep her a multigenerational favorite."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who plays 'Crazy Eyes' in Orange Is the New Black?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Uzo Aduba (born 1981) is an actress who won two Emmy Awards playing Suzanne 'Crazy Eyes' Warren in Orange Is the New Black, then a third Emmy playing Shirley Chisholm in Mrs. America. Her memoir The Road Is Good traces her Nigerian-American upbringing."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who plays The Color Purple's Sofia on Broadway and in the 2023 film?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Danielle Brooks (born 1989) is an actress and singer who earned a Tony nomination playing Sofia in The Color Purple on Broadway, then an Oscar nomination playing Sofia in the 2023 film \u2014 one of few performers to win acclaim in the same role in both versions."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who plays Monica Rambeau in the Marvel movies?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Teyonah Parris (born 1987) is an actress who plays Monica Rambeau across Marvel's WandaVision and The Marvels, after breaking through in Dear White People, Mad Men, and the film Chi-Raq. She trained at the Juilliard School in New York."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who stars in Judas and the Black Messiah and The Woman King era films?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Dominique Fishback (born 1991) is a Brooklyn actress acclaimed for playing activist Deborah Johnson in Judas and the Black Messiah, a young Beyonc\u00e9-inspired fan in the series Swarm, and a lead in Transformers: Rise of the Beasts. She writes and performs her own one-woman play."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who plays Meg in A Wrinkle in Time?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Storm Reid (born 2003) is an actress who at 14 played Meg Murry, the hero of Ava DuVernay's A Wrinkle in Time, and plays Gia, sister of Zendaya's Rue, in Euphoria. She won an Emmy for her guest role in The Last of Us while a student at USC."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who became a producer at 14, the youngest in Hollywood history?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Marsai Martin (born 2004) is an actress who plays Diane on Black-ish and in 2019, at 14, became the youngest executive producer in Hollywood history with her film Little, which she also starred in. She runs her own production company, Genius Productions."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who plays grown-up Alex in Black-ish and stars in Grown-ish?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yara Shahidi (born 2000) is an actress who plays Zoey on Black-ish and its spin-off Grown-ish, which she also produces. A Harvard graduate and voting advocate, she played Tinker Bell in Disney's Peter Pan & Wendy."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who plays Okoye, leader of Wakanda's Dora Milaje?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Danai Gurira (born 1978) is a Zimbabwean-American actress and playwright who plays Okoye, general of the Dora Milaje, in Marvel's Black Panther films, and Michonne in The Walking Dead. Her play Eclipsed, about Liberian women, reached Broadway with an all-Black women cast and creative team."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who won an Oscar at 12 Years a Slave and starred in Black Panther?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Lupita Nyong'o (born 1983) is a Mexican-born Kenyan actress who won the Academy Award for her first film role, as Patsey in 12 Years a Slave, and plays Nakia in Black Panther. Her children's book Sulwe, about colorism and self-love, is a bestseller."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who plays Gamora in Guardians of the Galaxy and Neytiri in Avatar?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Zoe Salda\u00f1a (born 1978) is an actress of Dominican and Puerto Rican heritage who is the highest-grossing actress in film history, starring as Neytiri in Avatar, Gamora in Marvel's Guardians of the Galaxy, and Uhura in Star Trek. In 2025 she won the Oscar for Emilia P\u00e9rez."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who co-anchors ABC's 20/20 and reports for Good Morning America?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Deborah Roberts (born 1960) is an ABC News journalist, co-anchor of 20/20 and a longtime correspondent across ABC programs, reporting from around the world. She is married to NBC weatherman Al Roker and has written a bestselling book on life's lessons."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who anchors ABC News Live Prime?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Linsey Davis (born 1977) is an ABC News journalist who anchors ABC News Live Prime, the network's flagship streaming newscast, and moderated the 2024 presidential debate. She is also a children's book author whose titles celebrate curiosity and faith."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is ABC's senior national correspondent covering Congress?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Rachel Scott (born 1995) is ABC News' senior political correspondent covering Congress and campaigns, known for direct questioning of presidents and candidates that has repeatedly made national news. She joined ABC as a producer and rose to the Capitol beat in her twenties."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is ABC's chief justice correspondent?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Pierre Thomas (born 1960) is ABC News' chief justice correspondent, covering the Justice Department, FBI, and national law enforcement from Washington. A Virginia native, he has won Emmy and Peabody recognition for breaking coverage of major criminal justice stories."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who co-anchors GMA3?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "DeMarco Morgan (born 1979) is an ABC News journalist who co-anchors GMA3: What You Need to Know and reports across Good Morning America. A graduate of Jackson State University, an HBCU, he anchored in Los Angeles and Fresno before joining ABC."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who co-anchors Good Morning America's weekend edition?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Janai Norman is an ABC News journalist who co-anchors the weekend edition of Good Morning America and anchors on ABC News Live. Known for warm, family-centered segments on parenting and wellness, she is one of morning TV's rising Black anchors \u2014 with no Wikidata entity yet."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is MSNBC's justice and civil rights correspondent?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Trymaine Lee (born 1978) is an MSNBC correspondent and host whose reporting on race, guns, and poverty won a Pulitzer Prize as part of the New Orleans Times-Picayune's Hurricane Katrina team. His book A Thousand Ways to Die examines gun violence in Black America."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who hosted MSNBC's Cross Connection and co-hosts Native Land Pod?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Tiffany Cross (born 1979) is a political analyst who hosted MSNBC's The Cross Connection and co-hosts the Native Land Pod with Angela Rye and Andrew Gillum. She is the author of Say It Louder, on Black voters and media."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who co-hosts MSNBC's The Weekend and ran a White House office?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Symone Sanders (born 1989) co-hosts MSNBC's The Weekend and served as chief spokesperson for Vice President Kamala Harris and as national press secretary for Bernie Sanders' 2016 campaign \u2014 at 25, among the youngest ever in that role. She is a Nebraska native."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the CNN commentator and former South Carolina lawmaker?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Bakari Sellers (born 1984) is a CNN political commentator, lawyer, and former South Carolina legislator who, elected at 22, was the youngest Black elected official in America. His father was a civil rights activist wounded in the 1968 Orangeburg Massacre."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the CNN host and author who co-founded Dream Corps?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Van Jones (born 1968) is a CNN host and commentator, author, and co-founder of the Dream Corps, which works on criminal justice reform and green jobs. He served as a green jobs adviser in the Obama White House and won an Emmy for virtual reality journalism."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who anchors The Faulkner Focus on Fox News?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Harris Faulkner (born 1965) is a Fox News anchor hosting The Faulkner Focus and co-hosting Outnumbered, a six-time Emmy winner whose father was an Army aviator. Her prime-time town halls and interviews make her Fox's most prominent Black anchor."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was one of Fox News' longest-serving Black anchors?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Arthel Neville (born 1962) is a Fox News journalist who in the 1990s became the first Black woman to host a national entertainment news show, on E!, and has anchored at Fox for decades. She is the niece of Art Neville of the Neville Brothers, New Orleans music royalty."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who co-hosts Fox & Friends First and hosts on Fox News?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Lawrence Jones (born 1992), Lawrence B. Jones III, is a Fox News host \u2014 the youngest Black co-host of Fox & Friends, joining its couch full-time in 2023 \u2014 who started as a teenage city-government activist in Garland, Texas, and reports from communities across the country for Fox."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the MSNBC political analyst who hosts her own SiriusXM and digital shows?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Zerlina Maxwell (born 1981) is a political analyst, MSNBC contributor, and host who served as a senior staff member on Hillary Clinton's 2016 campaign. She is the author of The End of White Politics and a leading voice on race, gender, and media online."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the professor hosting The Karen Hunter Show?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Karen Hunter (born 1965) is a journalist, professor, and host of The Karen Hunter Show on SiriusXM Urban View, a professor of practice at Hunter College. A Pulitzer Prize-winning former Daily News journalist and publisher, she runs a popular YouTube channel of news talk and interviews."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who hosted Bad Faith podcast and was a top Bernie Sanders surrogate?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Briahna Joy Gray (born 1985) is a lawyer and political commentator who served as national press secretary for Bernie Sanders' 2020 campaign, co-hosted The Hill's Rising, and hosts the Bad Faith podcast, one of the leading independent left politics shows online."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the lawyer-commentator behind The Reecie Colbert Show on YouTube?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Reecie Colbert is a political commentator and strategist who hosts The Reecie Colbert Show on YouTube and SiriusXM, known for data-driven breakdowns of Black voter behavior and election maps. A former federal budget analyst, she is one of independent Black media's fastest-growing voices \u2014 with no Wikidata entity yet."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the movement lawyer-commentator known as Olay?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Olayemi Olurin, known online as Olay, is a New York movement lawyer and political commentator whose YouTube and social videos translating court cases, policing, and immigration news for Black audiences reach millions. Her explainer style made her a defining voice of lawyer-commentators online."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who is the YouTube essayist F.D Signifier?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "F.D Signifier, born Fiqh Daniels, is a YouTube video essayist whose long-form essays on Black masculinity, hip-hop, film, and media \u2014 made from his background as a sociology educator in Atlanta \u2014 draw millions of views. He has no Wikidata entity or Wikipedia page yet: a clear Knowledge Graph gap."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who founded Bad Boy Records?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Sean Combs (born 1969), known as Diddy and Puff Daddy, is a Harlem-born rapper and producer who founded Bad Boy Records and built acts including the Notorious B.I.G. and Mary J. Blige into hip-hop's mainstream. In 2025 he was convicted on federal prostitution-related charges and sentenced to prison."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who sang 'I Believe I Can Fly'?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "R. Kelly (born Robert Sylvester Kelly, 1967, Chicago) is an R&B singer-songwriter behind 'I Believe I Can Fly' and a defining sound of 1990s R&B. Convicted in federal courts in 2021 and 2022 of racketeering, sex trafficking, and child sex crimes, he is serving a 30-year prison sentence."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who recorded 'Rocket 88'?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Ike Turner (1931\u20132007) was a Mississippi-born guitarist and bandleader whose 'Rocket 88' (1951) is cited as the first rock and roll record, and who led the Ike & Tina Turner Revue. His documented abuse of Tina Turner and a cocaine conviction define the darker half of his record."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the Queen of Rock 'n' Roll?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Tina Turner (1939\u20132023), born Anna Mae Bullock in Tennessee, was a singer of unmatched stage power: fronting the Ike & Tina Turner Revue, surviving Ike Turner's documented abuse, then returning solo with Private Dancer (1984) to become a global stadium star, inducted into the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame twice."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who played Andre Johnson on Black-ish?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Anthony Anderson (born 1970 in Los Angeles, raised in Compton) is an actor and comedian best known as Andre 'Dre' Johnson on Black-ish (2014\u20132022). A marquee live host, he has emceed the 75th Primetime Emmys, nine NAACP Image Awards ceremonies, and the Soul Train Awards in 2013 and 2026."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the largest museum dedicated to African American history?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The National Museum of African American History and Culture (opened 2016, Washington, DC) is the Smithsonian's museum devoted exclusively to African American life, history, and culture \u2014 the largest of its kind, holding some 40,000 objects at the foot of the Washington Monument."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the oldest independent Black history museum in the United States?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The DuSable Black History Museum in Chicago, founded in 1961 by artist and scholar Margaret Burroughs in her Bronzeville home, is regarded as the nation's oldest independent museum of Black history \u2014 named for Jean Baptiste Point du Sable, the Haitian-born founder of Chicago."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Where is the Charles H. Wright Museum?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Charles H. Wright Museum of African American History in Detroit, founded in 1965 by physician Charles H. Wright, is one of the world's largest museums dedicated to the African American experience, anchoring its story in the permanent exhibition And Still We Rise."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What was the first African American museum built by a major city?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The African American Museum in Philadelphia, opened in 1976 for the Bicentennial, was the first institution built and funded by a major American city to preserve and interpret African American history and culture \u2014 founded, in its words, because the story had to be told by its own."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is MoAD in San Francisco?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Museum of the African Diaspora (MoAD), opened in San Francisco in 2005, traces Black cultural expression across the whole global diaspora \u2014 from Africa through the Americas, the Caribbean, and Europe \u2014 beginning its visitor journey with the question: when did you first realize you were Black?"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Where was the National Civil Rights Museum built?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The National Civil Rights Museum, opened in 1991 in Memphis, Tennessee, is built at the Lorraine Motel \u2014 the site of Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.'s assassination on April 4, 1968 \u2014 preserving Room 306 and tracing the civil rights movement from slavery to the present."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the Legacy Museum in Montgomery?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Legacy Museum in Montgomery, Alabama \u2014 opened in 2018 by the Equal Justice Initiative near a site where enslaved people were once warehoused \u2014 narrates America's history from enslavement through mass incarceration, standing near the National Memorial for Peace and Justice."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Where did the International African American Museum open?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The International African American Museum opened in 2023 in Charleston, South Carolina, at Gadsden's Wharf \u2014 where roughly 40 percent of enslaved Africans who survived the Middle Passage entered North America \u2014 grounding its galleries in that sacred, difficult ground."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the Smithsonian's African art museum?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The National Museum of African Art, founded in 1964 and part of the Smithsonian since 1979, is the United States' national museum of African art, holding collections that span centuries of the continent's visual traditions on the National Mall in Washington, DC."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is CAAM in Los Angeles?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The California African American Museum, founded in 1981 in Los Angeles's Exposition Park, documents the art, history, and culture of African Americans in California and the American West \u2014 a region of Black history too often left out of the national telling."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What does the Apartheid Museum in Johannesburg document?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Apartheid Museum, opened in 2001 in Johannesburg, South Africa, documents the rise and fall of apartheid \u2014 visitors receive entry cards randomly classifying them as white or non-white, experiencing the system's machinery of racial separation from the first doorway."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the largest museum of contemporary African art?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Zeitz MOCAA, opened in 2017 in Cape Town, South Africa, inside a carved-out historic grain silo, is the largest museum dedicated to contemporary art from Africa and its diaspora \u2014 a cathedral-scale statement that African art's present tense belongs in monumental space."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the Mus\u00e9e des Civilisations Noires in Dakar?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Museum of Black Civilizations, inaugurated in 2018 in Dakar, Senegal, realizes L\u00e9opold S\u00e9dar Senghor's decades-old vision of a museum for all Black civilizations worldwide \u2014 and has become a focal point in the campaign for restitution of African heritage held in Western museums."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What happened to District Six in Cape Town?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The District Six Museum, opened in 1994 in Cape Town, memorializes the multiracial community of District Six \u2014 declared a whites-only area in 1966 under apartheid, its more than 60,000 residents forcibly removed and its homes bulldozed, leaving the museum to hold the street signs and the memory."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the Black Cultural Archives in London?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Black Cultural Archives in Brixton, London \u2014 founded in 1981 by educator Len Garrison after the Brixton uprisings \u2014 is Britain's first Black heritage archive, opened in its own building on Windrush Square in 2014 to preserve the history of African and Caribbean people in the UK."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the AfricaMuseum in Belgium?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The AfricaMuseum in Tervuren, Belgium \u2014 opened in 1910 as a showcase of King Leopold II's Congo colony \u2014 holds one of the world's largest Central African collections; after a five-year renovation it reopened in 2018 confronting, in its own galleries, the colonial violence behind them."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the Stone of Hope in Washington, DC?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Martin Luther King Jr. Memorial, dedicated in 2011 on Washington's Tidal Basin, centers on the Stone of Hope \u2014 a 30-foot granite figure of Dr. King, sculpted by Lei Yixin, carved from the 'mountain of despair' named in his 1963 I Have a Dream speech."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Who was the first Black American honored in the US Capitol's Statuary Hall?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The statue of educator and civil rights leader Mary McLeod Bethune, unveiled in the US Capitol's National Statuary Hall in July 2022 representing Florida, made her the first African American honored with a state statue in the collection \u2014 sculpted by Nilda Comas from Italian marble."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Kehinde Wiley's Rumors of War?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Rumors of War (2019), Kehinde Wiley's first public sculpture, stands at the Virginia Museum of Fine Arts in Richmond: a young Black man in streetwear, mounted on a monumental bronze horse in the pose of the Confederate statues the city was then removing \u2014 answering them in their own language."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What replaced the Lee statue era in Richmond?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Emancipation and Freedom Monument, unveiled on Richmond's Brown's Island in September 2021, depicts a man and a woman with an infant, newly freed \u2014 sculpted by Thomas Jay Warren and installed blocks from where Robert E. Lee's statue had come down weeks before."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the National Memorial for Peace and Justice?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The National Memorial for Peace and Justice, opened in 2018 in Montgomery, Alabama, by the Equal Justice Initiative, is the nation's first memorial to lynching's victims: more than 800 steel columns hang overhead, one for each county where a racial terror lynching is documented."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is the Ark of Return at the United Nations?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Ark of Return, unveiled at United Nations headquarters in New York in 2015, is the UN's permanent memorial to the victims of slavery and the transatlantic slave trade \u2014 a triangular marble monument by Haitian-American architect Rodney Leon, sited where the world's diplomats must pass it."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Where is Alison Saar's Harriet Tubman statue?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Swing Low, Alison Saar's 13-foot bronze of Harriet Tubman, dedicated in 2008 in Harlem, New York, depicts Tubman striding forward with the roots of slavery pulling at her skirt \u2014 the first statue of a Black woman in a New York City public space in decades of monument building."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Where is the Denmark Vesey statue in Charleston?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Denmark Vesey Monument, unveiled in Charleston's Hampton Park in 2014 after a campaign of nearly four decades, honors the free Black carpenter who planned a major slave revolt in 1822 and was executed for it \u2014 debated in Charleston for generations before the city let him stand."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What was rediscovered at the African Burial Ground in New York?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The African Burial Ground National Monument in Lower Manhattan marks the rediscovery, during a 1991 construction dig, of a colonial-era cemetery holding more than 15,000 free and enslaved Africans \u2014 the largest of its kind in North America, now a national monument with a memorial by Rodney Leon."
   }
  },
